{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 32, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterclass in storytelling, direction, and performance. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the film chronicles the founding of facebook and the ensuing legal battles that followed, but it’s far more than a simple biopic—it’s a sharp, incisive exploration of ambition, betrayal, and the cost of innovation in the digital age.\n\njesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, capturing the character’s social awkwardness, intellectual brilliance, and emotional detachment with chilling precision. his portrayal is not a caricature but a nuanced study of a man who is brilliant yet emotionally stunted, driven by a need for validation he can never quite attain. andy serkis, as the wizened, weary attorney, and jesse plemons, as the loyal but sidelined dustin moskovitz, provide strong supporting turns, but the real standout is jesse eisenberg’s ability to make the audience both sympathize with and recoil from his character.\n\nthe screenplay by aaron sorkin is a marvel of pacing and dialogue. every line crackles with wit, tension, and subtext. sorkin’s trademark rapid-fire exchanges aren’t just clever—they reveal character, motive, and power dynamics with surgical precision. the film’s structure, told through two parallel depositions, creates a compelling narrative frame that keeps the audience guessing about truth, memory, and perspective.\n\ndavid fincher’s direction is cold, precise, and visually arresting. the use of shadows, tight close-ups, and a muted color palette reflects the emotional isolation of the characters. the cinematography by jeff cronenweth is stark and beautiful, and the score by trent reznor and atticus ross is revolutionary—electronic, haunting, and perfectly aligned with the film’s themes of alienation and technological obsession.\n\nthe film also succeeds as a cultural document. it doesn’t take sides in the legal disputes between zuckerberg and the winklevoss twins or sean parker, but it forces the viewer to question who really owns an idea, and at what cost progress is made. it’s a portrait of american capitalism, privilege, and the birth of a new social order built on code and connection.\n\nthe social network is not a feel-good story. it’s a sobering, often uncomfortable look at how genius can be lonely, how friendship can be transactional, and how the pursuit of connection can lead to profound isolation. it’s a film that lingers long after the credits roll, asking not just what facebook became, but what it says about us. a modern classic.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0003810394846368581}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.4176788032054901}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -1.371579885482788}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": " storytelling", "logprob": -0.7669656872749329}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -3.218599158572033e-05}, {"token": " direction", "logprob": -0.04766642302274704}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " performance", "logprob": -0.5005567073822021}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -1.3451298475265503}, {"token": " directed", "logprob": -0.0011269653914496303}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -1.0967194612021558e-05}, {"token": " david", "logprob": -0.0007425173535011709}, {"token": " fin", "logprob": -0.001839375589042902}, {"token": "cher", "logprob": -8.940656698541716e-06}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.03028048202395439}, {"token": " written", "logprob": -0.0025137036573141813}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -2.7418097943154862e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0